Yes, such a set of $n$ non-intersecting segments must always exist.

## Proof

Let $R = \{r_1, \dots, r_n\}$ be the red points and $B = \{b_1, \dots, b_n\}$ the blue points. A **matching** is a bijection $\sigma : \{1,\dots,n\} \to \{1,\dots,n\}$, producing the $n$ segments $\overline{r_i b_{\sigma(i)}}$. There are finitely many matchings ($n!$ of them), so among all matchings we may choose one $\sigma$ that **minimizes the total length**

$$L(\sigma) = \sum_{i=1}^{n} |r_i\, b_{\sigma(i)}|.$$

We claim that in this length-minimizing matching, no two of the $n$ segments intersect (except possibly at shared endpoints, which cannot occur here since a red point is paired to exactly one blue point).

Suppose for contradiction that two segments $\overline{r_i\, b_{\sigma(i)}}$ and $\overline{r_j\, b_{\sigma(j)}}$ ($i \neq j$) cross at an interior point $P$. Since the four endpoints are distinct and no three points of $A$ are collinear, $P$ is distinct from all four endpoints and lies strictly inside both segments. Thus

$$|r_i\, b_{\sigma(i)}| = |r_i P| + |P\, b_{\sigma(i)}|, \qquad |r_j\, b_{\sigma(j)}| = |r_j P| + |P\, b_{\sigma(j)}|.$$

Now consider the "swapped" matching $\sigma'$ that agrees with $\sigma$ everywhere except $\sigma'(i) = \sigma(j)$ and $\sigma'(j) = \sigma(i)$, i.e., we replace the two crossing segments by $\overline{r_i\, b_{\sigma(j)}}$ and $\overline{r_j\, b_{\sigma(i)}}$.

By the triangle inequality applied to the broken paths through $P$:

$$|r_i\, b_{\sigma(j)}| \le |r_i P| + |P\, b_{\sigma(j)}|, \qquad |r_j\, b_{\sigma(i)}| \le |r_j P| + |P\, b_{\sigma(i)}|.$$

We show at least one of these is **strict**. If $|r_i\, b_{\sigma(j)}| = |r_i P| + |P\, b_{\sigma(j)}|$, then $r_i, P, b_{\sigma(j)}$ are collinear. But $P$ lies on line $r_i b_{\sigma(i)}$, so $r_i, P, b_{\sigma(i)}$ are collinear; together this forces $r_i, b_{\sigma(i)}, b_{\sigma(j)}$ to be collinear — three points of $A$ on one line, contradicting the general-position hypothesis. Hence the first inequality is strict. (Likewise the second is strict by the symmetric argument using $r_i, r_j, b_{\sigma(i)}$.) Therefore

$$|r_i\, b_{\sigma(j)}| + |r_j\, b_{\sigma(i)}| < |r_i P| + |P\, b_{\sigma(j)}| + |r_j P| + |P\, b_{\sigma(i)}| = |r_i\, b_{\sigma(i)}| + |r_j\, b_{\sigma(j)}|.$$

All other segments are unchanged, so $L(\sigma') < L(\sigma)$, contradicting the minimality of $\sigma$.

Hence no two segments in the minimal matching cross. This minimal matching provides the desired $n$ pairwise non-intersecting segments, each joining one red point to one blue point. $\blacksquare$

